WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.379
A few days after the gathering at Ruby's, Anne and Diana were walking slowly near the stream next to Green Gables.

2
00:00:07.379 --> 00:00:10.186
"What are you thinking, Anne?" asked Diana.

3
00:00:10.186 --> 00:00:14.779
"I've been thinking about writing a short story," Anne confessed.

4
00:00:14.779 --> 00:00:19.254
"I'm hoping I can get it published, in a women's magazine maybe."

5
00:00:19.254 --> 00:00:24.599
"Oh, have you started it?" exclaimed Diana, very interested.

6
00:00:24.599 --> 00:00:30.702
"I've only written a few pages, but I've got a suitable plot," Anne replied.

7
00:00:30.702 --> 00:00:37.282
"The main character's name is Averil. The hero's name is Harold Jonathon Wittington.

8
00:00:37.282 --> 00:00:41.721
I think they are elegant and fitting names for the main characters, don't you?

9
00:00:41.721 --> 00:00:45.167
It took me hours to think of those names, Diana."

10
00:00:45.167 --> 00:00:49.377
"Have you named all the characters?" asked Diana wistfully.

11
00:00:49.377 --> 00:00:56.298
"If you haven't, could I name someone? Even if it's just some unimportant person."

12
00:00:56.298 --> 00:00:59.972
"You can name the servant," conceded Anne.

13
00:00:59.972 --> 00:01:05.042
"I think his name will be . . . Bobby," said Diana promptly.

14
00:01:05.042 --> 00:01:07.977
"And you'll let me read your story, won't you, Anne?"

15
00:01:07.977 --> 00:01:16.434
"When it is finished," Anne said, "I'll read it to you and Mr. Harrison." He was Anne's neighbor in Avonlea.

16
00:01:16.434 --> 00:01:24.517
"And I want you both to criticize it severely, since no one else shall see it until it is published."

17
00:01:24.517 --> 00:01:30.851
"How are you going to end the story? Happily or unhappily?" Diana asked.

18
00:01:30.851 --> 00:01:39.327
"I'm not sure," Anne said. "I'd like it to end unhappily, because that would be so much more romantic.

19
00:01:39.327 --> 00:01:44.511
But I think magazine editors have a prejudice against sad endings. A

20
00:01:44.511 --> 00:01:55.488
famous author once said that only a genius should write an unhappy ending. And I am anything but a genius." "Oh,

21
00:01:55.488 --> 00:01:58.435
I like happy endings best. I

22
00:01:58.435 --> 00:02:08.186
think the hero should marry Averil," said Diana, who, especially since her engagement to Fred, thought this was how every story should end. "But

23
00:02:08.186 --> 00:02:12.461
don't you like to cry when you read stories?" Anne asked. "Yes,"

24
00:02:12.461 --> 00:02:21.949
Diana said. "But I only like crying in the middle of the story. I like everything to end happily." "Hmm,

25
00:02:21.949 --> 00:02:28.256
perhaps I should have one tragic scene," said Anne thoughtfully. "Perhaps

26
00:02:28.256 --> 00:02:35.552
Bobby the servant is injured in a tractor accident in the fields and dies—" "No!

27
00:02:35.552 --> 00:02:40.710
You can't kill Bobby!" said Diana, laughing. "He

28
00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:46.206
belongs to me and I want him to live and flourish! Kill someone else." "But

29
00:02:46.206 --> 00:02:51.642
it's my story!" Anne laughed with her. Over

30
00:02:51.642 --> 00:02:56.970
the next fortnight, Anne worked hard on her story, writing day and night. She

31
00:02:56.970 --> 00:03:02.047
managed to include a tragic scene without killing Bobby the servant. When

32
00:03:02.047 --> 00:03:07.540
the story was finally finished, Anne read it to Diana on the porch of Green Gables. When

33
00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:14.028
Anne looked up after reading the last paragraph, Diana seemed disappointed. "Why

34
00:03:14.028 --> 00:03:18.451
did you kill Anthony Lennox?" Diana asked reproachfully. "He

35
00:03:18.451 --> 00:03:23.965
was the villain," protested Anne. "He had to be punished!" "I

36
00:03:23.965 --> 00:03:29.341
liked him best of all!" said Diana, unreasonably, it seemed to Anne. "Well,

37
00:03:29.341 --> 00:03:34.854
he's dead, and he'll have to stay dead," said Anne, annoyed. "If

38
00:03:34.854 --> 00:03:43.739
I had let him live, he would have kept persecuting Averil and Harold. The villain had to die, Diana!" "Well,

39
00:03:43.739 --> 00:03:51.985
anyway," Diana said, "it's a wonderful story, Anne, and it will make you famous, I'm sure. What's its title?" "Oh,

40
00:03:51.985 --> 00:03:59.340
I decided that long ago," Anne replied. "It's called ‘Averil's Atonement.' Both

41
00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:03.916
words begin with the letter a , so it sounds sophisticated.

42
00:04:03.916 --> 00:04:10.134
Now, Diana, tell me honestly, do you see any faults in my story?"

43
00:04:10.134 --> 00:04:20.099
"Well," hesitated Diana, "that part where Averil makes the cake doesn't seem quite romantic enough to match the rest of the story.

44
00:04:20.099 --> 00:04:22.181
Heroines shouldn't cook."

45
00:04:22.181 --> 00:04:27.789
"But that's a funny part, and one of the best parts of the story!" Anne exclaimed.

46
00:04:27.789 --> 00:04:35.775
Diana prudently refrained from any further criticism, but later Mr. Harrison criticized the story a lot.

47
00:04:35.775 --> 00:04:41.259
"Why did you write a story about rich people with silly names?" he asked.

48
00:04:41.259 --> 00:04:45.155
"You should write about the people you know, here in Avonlea."

49
00:04:45.155 --> 00:04:49.660
"But Avonlea is not romantic," Anne protested.

50
00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:55.266
"Well," Mr. Harrison replied, "I don't like the hero, Harold, either.

51
00:04:55.266 --> 00:05:04.967
He's too good . He just stands around being polite and romantic. I prefer the villain, Anthony

52
00:05:04.967 --> 00:05:10.121
Lennox." Anne decided the next time she wrote a story, she wouldn't ask for anyone's

53
00:05:10.121 --> 00:05:17.427
feedback! Anne sent the story to one of the biggest women's magazines and waited to hear from them. She didn't have to wait

54
00:05:17.427 --> 00:05:25.668
long. The magazine sent the story back a week later with a letter that said "Averil's Atonement" wasn't "suitable."

55
00:05:25.668 --> 00:05:32.618
Diana tried to comfort Anne, saying, "Those big magazines are no good anyway," and that she should keep trying.

56
00:05:32.618 --> 00:05:37.091
Not to be dissuaded, Anne sent the story to another magazine.

57
00:05:37.091 --> 00:05:42.610
It was sent back even faster. Anne didn't believe the editor had read it at all!

58
00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:49.819
Discouraged, she put her story away underneath her dresser and gave the extra copy to Diana.

59
00:05:49.819 --> 00:05:57.330
Anne never mentioned the two rejections to Mr. Harrison, but one evening he asked her bluntly if her story had been accepted.

60
00:05:57.330 --> 00:06:01.995
"No, the editors didn't accept it," Anne answered briefly.

61
00:06:01.995 --> 00:06:05.218
Mr. Harrison looked at Anne's flushed face.

62
00:06:05.218 --> 00:06:09.834
"Well, I hope you keep writing, Anne," he said encouragingly.

63
00:06:09.834 --> 00:06:15.283
"No, I shall never try to write a story again," declared Anne.

64
00:06:15.283 --> 00:06:21.722
"I wouldn't give up altogether," said Mr. Harrison gently, looking at Anne's flushed face.

65
00:06:21.722 --> 00:06:24.390
"I would keep writing once in a while.

66
00:06:24.390 --> 00:06:27.799
Why don't you write about people and places you know?

67
00:06:27.799 --> 00:06:36.580
Make the characters talk in everyday English, and write about everyday life. But don't stop writing, Anne."

68
00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:41.329
"No. It was foolish of me to try," Anne said firmly.

69
00:06:41.329 --> 00:06:46.171
"I am good at teaching, and when I finish college, I'll return to that.

70
00:06:46.171 --> 00:06:49.940
I can teach. I can't write stories."

71
00:06:49.940 --> 00:06:55.043
"When you finish college, it'll be time to get married, Anne," said Mr. Harrison.

72
00:06:55.043 --> 00:07:07.915
Anne scowled. Sometimes Mr. Harrison was so annoying! She got up and marched home.

